# Browser tests in {{.Name}}

Repos' system tests drive Chromium in the `playwright` service, through its
server at `PLAYWRIGHT_SERVER_URL` (`ws://playwright:3000/`). The browser is
in that container, not the repo's, so two things differ from a laptop:

- **The app under test** runs in the repo's container. Its test server
  listens on `0.0.0.0`, and the browser reaches it by the repo's compose
  name, `SYSTEM_TESTS_HOST` (such as `http://shop:<port>`), never
  `localhost`.
- **Hosts the app serves**, such as `acme.example.com` or
  `acme.shop.localhost`, resolve nowhere in that container, and the
  Playwright server drops Chromium flags such as `--host-resolver-rules`.
  So the browser sends every request through a proxy instead: see below.

## Run them

```sh
neptuno exec <repo> -- bin/rails test:system
neptuno exec <repo> -- bin/rails test test/system/checkout_test.rb:42
```

`neptuno up <repo>` or `exec` starts the `playwright` service too. If the
app returns 502s right after `neptuno restart`, wait until
`http://<repo>.localhost/up` answers before running tests.

## Subdomains and custom domains

Rails repos made with the `playwright` option have
`test/application_system_test_case.rb`, which picks the test server's port
up front and starts a small proxy in the test process. The browser uses it
as its HTTP proxy, passed to the Playwright server in the
`launch-options` of the server URL, and it hands every request to the app
under test whatever its host, WebSockets (Action Cable) included. So tests
visit real-looking hosts with no DNS:

```ruby
visit "http://acme.example.com/"          # Capybara adds the port
assert_text "Acme"
```

Rails sees `request.host == "acme.example.com"`, so subdomain and
custom-domain routing work as in production. Point any URL the app builds
for itself (mailer `default_url_options`, tenant domains) at the same hosts.

The catch: outside sites (CDNs, fonts, third-party scripts) reach the app
too, and HTTPS ones fail. The proxy is set by env in `environments/<repo>/`
or per run:

| Env | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `SYSTEM_TESTS_PROXY=off` | no proxy; only `SYSTEM_TESTS_HOST` reaches the app |
| `SYSTEM_TESTS_PROXY_BYPASS=*.googleapis.com,cdn.example.net` | those hosts go direct, comma-separated, `*` wildcards allowed |

For a repo that predates this, copy the top half of that file (the
`SystemTestsProxy` class and the `register_driver` block) from a new app
made with `neptuno add <name> --stack rails --with playwright`.

### RSpec

The same setup works in `spec/support/system.rb`: keep the proxy class,
`register_driver`, `server_host`, `app_host` and `always_include_port` as
they are, and drive it per system spec:

```ruby
RSpec.configure do |config|
  config.before(type: :system) { driven_by :playwright_server }
end
```

### Other stacks

The idea carries over: pick the test server's port, run a proxy that
forwards every request (and tunnels `CONNECT`) to it, and connect with
`PLAYWRIGHT_SERVER_URL` plus
`?launch-options={"proxy":{"server":"http://<repo>:<proxy port>"}}`
(URL-encoded). Node's `chromium.connect()` takes that URL as is.

## Traces

Each system test records a Playwright trace: every action with a screenshot,
the DOM, console and network. They land in
`repos/<repo>/tmp/playwright-traces/<test>.zip` with a `.json` beside it,
and the dashboard's Traces list or
`http://playwright.localhost/trace/` opens them.

| `PLAYWRIGHT_TRACE` | Keeps |
| --- | --- |
| `failed` (default) | traces of tests that fail or error, and of tests declared with `trace: true` |
| `all` | every test's trace |
| `off` | none, and nothing is recorded |

To keep a passing test's trace without changing the env, tag it:

```ruby
test "checkout", trace: true do
  # ...
end
```

In RSpec, `it "checks out", trace: true do` with the same check in the
`after` hook that saves traces: `example.metadata[:trace]`.

## When it doesn't work

1. `Errno::ECONNREFUSED` or a blank page at `http://<repo>:<port>`: the
   test server isn't on `0.0.0.0`, or `SYSTEM_TESTS_HOST` isn't the repo's
   compose name. Check with `neptuno env show <repo>`.
2. A connection error about the Playwright version: the test client must
   match the server's minor version (`playwright-ruby-client` pins it).
3. An outside asset fails to load: add its host to
   `SYSTEM_TESTS_PROXY_BYPASS`.
4. The browser hits the wrong server for a host: the proxy is off, or the
   test builds a URL with an explicit port other than Capybara's.
